+# Why every field is a pill — `--radius-field`
+
+## What consumes the variable
+
+Scaffold-ETH 2 styles come from **DaisyUI 5** (running on Tailwind v4).
+DaisyUI 5 exposes three theme-level radius tokens, set inside each
+`@plugin "daisyui/theme"` block:
+
+- `--radius-selector` — checkboxes, radios, toggles, badges
+- `--radius-field` — **input, select, textarea, file-input, and buttons** (the "field-sized" controls)
+- `--radius-box` — cards, modals, alerts (the "box-sized" surfaces)
+
+Every DaisyUI field component rule sets its corner radius *from that token*, not from a literal. Roughly:
+
+```css
+.input, .select, .textarea, .file-input { border-radius: var(--radius-field); }
+```
+
+So the value you wrote once —
+
+```css
+--radius-field: 9999rem;
+```
+
+is read by **every** `.input`, `.select`, `.textarea` (and buttons) in the app, on every page. `border-radius` is capped by the browser at half the shorter side, so a huge value like `9999rem` always resolves to "fully rounded" → **pill ends** on single-line controls.
+
+On a multi-line `textarea` the same enormous radius is clamped to half the box *height*; the resulting quarter-circle corners are large enough to reach into the text area, so the first/last lines get **clipped at the corners**. That's the same root cause, not a separate bug.
+
+## Why the per-component `rounded-*` patching keeps losing
+
+The teammate's patches (`rounded-lg` on two inputs) fight the symptom, not the source:
+
+1. **It's whack-a-mole against the token, not the token.** The pill shape isn't authored per-component — it's emitted by DaisyUI's component rules *from the theme variable*. So every field is pill-shaped **by default**. Every new form field the team adds inherits `var(--radius-field)` = `9999rem` and comes out pill again. You'd have to remember to hand-patch every field forever, including ones added by libraries/`scaffold-eth` components you don't control.
+
+2. **The patches drift.** A hardcoded `rounded-lg` freezes one field at Tailwind's `0.5rem` while the theme's intended radius lives in `--radius-field`. Change the theme later and the token-driven fields move while the patched ones stay put — so the "fixed" inputs are exactly the ones that fall out of sync with the rest of the app.
+
+3. **It only ever covers `light`.** Even a perfectly patched field is styled per-*instance*, so it does nothing for the other theme block — see below.
+
+Fixing the variable fixes the class of problem; patching components fixes one instance and creates a maintenance debt.
+
+## The correct fix
+
+Set the token to a real corner radius instead of the pill sentinel. Match the value the team was already reaching for with `rounded-lg` (Tailwind v4 `rounded-lg` = `0.5rem`) so the patched fields and everything else converge:
+
+```css
+--radius-field: 0.5rem;
+```
+
+(Use whatever the design calls for — `0.375rem`/`rounded-md` or `0.25rem`/`rounded` are equally valid; the point is a small finite value, not `9999rem`.)
+
+### Every place in this file it must be applied
+
+The radius tokens are **per-theme** — each `@plugin "daisyui/theme"` block declares its own copy. Setting it in one block leaves the others pill-shaped. So change it in **every theme block in `globals.css`**, not just `light`:
+
+```css
+@plugin "daisyui/theme" {
+  name: "light";
+  ...
+  --radius-field: 0.5rem;   /* was 9999rem */
+  ...
+}
+
+@plugin "daisyui/theme" {
+  name: "dark";
+  ...
+  --radius-field: 0.5rem;   /* was 9999rem — must match */
+  ...
+}
+```
+
+Scaffold-ETH 2 ships `light` and `dark`; apply it to both (and to any additional custom theme block present). While you're there, sanity-check `--radius-selector` and `--radius-box` for the same `9999rem` sentinel if badges/cards also look wrong.
+
+### Then remove the patches
+
+Once the token is correct, delete the per-component `rounded-lg` overrides. They're now redundant, and leaving them in re-introduces the drift problem — they'd pin two fields to a literal while the rest follow the theme. With the token fixed, every current and future field gets the right radius automatically, from one place.

